  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather is expected through the middle\nof the week with climbing temperatures.\n\nLow overcast will persist through tonight with some spotty\ndrizzle here and there. Some patchy fog is possible west of the\nBlue Ridge Mountains, but widespread low clouds will likely\nprevent fog from becoming dense and/or widespread. Residual \nmoisture may remain trapped beneath the subsidence inversion \nwith weak gradients on Tuesday, although deeper mixing will \nlikely support more breaks of sun. \n\nWith ridging building in at the sfc and aloft and light sfc\nwinds, pattern will become more conducive for fog development, \nif skies clear enough. This appears more likely to happen late \nTue night into Wed morning. \n\nTemperatures will climb further Wednesday and Thursday, with \nmore locations reaching the 80s. Some spots could even reach the\nupper 80s by Thursday. Developing southerly flow will lead to \nmore sunshine. Dry weather is expected both days. With light \nwinds and elevated dew points, some patchy fog could form during\nthe overnight hours.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will approach on Friday, leading to\ncooler and potentially unsettled weather for the weekend.\n\nSurface high pressure begins to slide offshore Thursday into Friday. \nAbove-normal temperatures likely persist into Friday afternoon with \ntemperatures approaching the upper-80s as upper-level ridging slowly \nbreaks down ahead of an incoming cold front from the Great Lakes. \nThis front could slowly begin to pass through the Mid-Atlantic by \nFriday evening before stalling just off to the south. \n\nThis front could potentailly linger throughout the weekend before \nfinally pushing offshore towards the start of next week. This will \nlead to a much cooler weekend overall, along with chances for \nintermittent showers as the front lingers over the Mid-\nAtlantic. Rainfall probabilities remain lower (20%-40%) at this time\ngiven the weak nature of the front and remaining uncertainty in the \nmodel guidance. As this front pushes offshore, conditions could \nbegin to clear early next week, with daily highs returning to the \n60s and lower 70s in the wake of the front.",
